{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission-review-c1-full.json\", \"summary\": \"Turn 70 reviewed the full-history scenario, which removes the prepared-record limitation from the headline finding.\\n\\nState on entry. The strengthened artifact, 6c0ec62d566a4fbabe5ffda02b0fff75 (executed as e8ad6c82…), reported violated with the oracle correspondence unreviewed. Its retained stream shows the driven history first — proposal_committed with leader_status 4, third_status 4 and command_is_put true, meaning replica 1, the instance owner and leader, admitted a client proposal through handlePropose, ran a real PreAccept round with replica 2, fast-committed and broadcast the Commit that replica 2 installed — and then the recovery of the same instance by replica 0, which lacks any record because the harness kept it out of the proposal's alive set.\\n\\nReview judgement (checker_correspondence, no_issue_found). Requirement versus predicate: the same three properties encode the obligation, and the run reports adopted_committed false, vectors_match false and all_committed_match false together with applied_match false, all evaluated on printed fields. Entry and legality: the committed value now comes from the implementation itself rather than from an installed record, so the scenario is a legal history in which one replica holds the instance COMMITTED with a real command and another has never seen it; the recovering replica then enters recovery directly, which remains the declared substitution, and the timeout that would trigger it in production is separately confirmed (claim C8). Oracle and identity: the reply the resolution ignores is emitted by the committed replica's own handler, and the instance identity, predicates and monitors are the reviewed ones. What this changes: the limitation the earlier reviews recorded — that the peer's COMMITTED record was prepared — no longer applies to this scenario, while the remaining limits are unchanged and stated: the direct recovery entry, the N=3 Thrifty configuration, and the harness-created default record on the recovering replica (justified because that replica received no message about the instance).\\n\\nEffect. The C1 agreement finding now stands on a driven proposal, commit and recovery history, with its propagation and applied-state consequences reproduced from that history, alongside the untouched promoted-leader variant C6 and the other eight settled claim families. Audit spec version 14; the two paused candidates and the recorded open items are unchanged, and no requirement, premise or earlier observation was weakened.\"}"}}
